is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router2
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: FRRouting (FRR) routing stack on router2, specifically zebra and bgpd. The management CLI (vtysh) depends on these daemons and fails when they are down.

Mechanism: With bgpd on router2 down, the eBGP session on router1 has no peer and sits in Active state, so router1 withdraws 200.1.1.0/24. As a result, packets from pc1 (195.11.14.0/24) toward pc2 (200.1.1.0/24) are immediately unroutable at router1, producing “Destination Net Unreachable” from 195.11.14.1. pc2 can still ping its default gateway (router2: 200.1.1.1), confirming local L2/L3 are fine.

Localization and evidence:
- router2: systemctl shows frr inactive (dead); vtysh fails (exit 1); frr_show_ip_route says “zebra is not running”; frr_get_bgp_conf says “bgpd is not running.” Process list lacks zebra/bgpd (only mgmtd/staticd present).
- router1: BGP summary shows neighbor 193.10.11.2 in Active, PfxRcd 0; routing table has no 200.1.1.0/24.
- pc1→pc2 ping returns “From 195.11.14.1 Destination Net Unreachable,” proving router1 lacks a route to 200.1.1.0/24.

Root cause: FRR daemons (zebra and bgpd) stopped/crashed on router2, bringing down the BGP peering.

Corrective fix: Restart FRR on router2 and ensure watchfrr manages zebra and bgpd. Verify /etc/frr/daemons has zebra=yes and bgpd=yes and /etc/frr/frr.conf contains the BGP config. After starting, confirm BGP establishes on router1 (Established state) and routes 200.1.1.0/24 and 195.11.14.0/24 are exchanged, restoring pc1↔pc2 connectivity.
=== END ===